    \u0438\u043d\u0442\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0441\u043e\u0432\u0430\u0442\u044c\u0441\u044f \u0430\u043b\u0445\u0438\u043c\u0438\u0435\u0439,
    \u043a\u043e\u0442\u043e\u0440\u0430\u044f \u0431\u044b\u043b\u0430 \u0434\u043b\u044f \u043d\u0435\u0433\u043e
    \u043d\u0435 \u043c\u0430\u0433\u0438\u0435\u0439, \u0430 \u0440\u0430\u043d\u043d\u0435\u0439
    \u0445\u0438\u043c\u0438\u0435\u0439. \u0415\u0433\u043e \u0436\u0438\u0437\u043d\u044c
    \u2014 \u044d\u0442\u043e \u0431\u0435\u0441\u043a\u043e\u043d\u0435\u0447\u043d\u044b\u0439
    \u0447\u0435\u0440\u043d\u043e\u0432\u0438\u043a \u0432\u0435\u043b\u0438\u043a\u043e\u0439
    \u043a\u043d\u0438\u0433\u0438, \u043a\u043e\u0442\u043e\u0440\u0443\u044e \u043e\u043d
    \u0442\u0430\u043a \u0438 \u043d\u0435 \u0443\u0441\u043f\u0435\u043b \u0434\u043e\u043f\u0438\u0441\u0430\u0442\u044c."
  interestingFactsEn: "The life of Gottfried Wilhelm Leibnitz is not only a series of genius discoveries, but also a collection of amazing, funny and even paradoxical stories. His personality was so multifaceted that it produced the most incredible rumours and chickens. Here is a collection of the most interesting facts about \"the last universal genius\":\n\n* Prophecies and wonders are a baptismal sign: His father, Professor Friedrich Leibnitz, was a man of observation and mysticism, and on the crucifixes of the baby Gottfried, he noticed that the newborn had looked up to the ceiling and looked at him closely.His father, who was in the room, took it as divine vision and ordered him to serve the grateful prayer, and, unfortunately, the savior's vision did not protect Friedrich himself — he died when Gothfrid was only six.\n\n* Reading as a sport - Self - Studyer at the age of four: Staying with a rich father's library, little Leibnitz did not wait for teachers. He learned to read from the illustrated publication of Titus Libya, where short signatures of Latin were under the pictures. Thus, by the age of four, he learned one of the most difficult languages in the world.For a teenager who had just entered the academic path, it was an incredible phenomenal memory and a literary gift.\n\n==Carees of Mathematics and Technology==Cureises of Mathematics and Technology - Wheel of Leibnica (staged valica): In creating its famous arhythmometer, Leibnitz invented a mechanism that could be considered to be a prototype of a modern hard disk. Instead of making complex machines, he made a cylinder with different lengths (9 steps) allowing the machine to multiply and divide as much as any previous device. He personally brought this miracle machine to London in 1673 and produced a furor.- Two as a stumbling block: His greatest mathematical discovery, differential calculation, was published in 1684 in an article that started with... typing! It was a mistake in the formula set. Fortunately, colleagues quickly learned the truth, but it was funny that the foundation of the new science almost destroyed the printing negligence.\n\nIn 1672, Leibnitz proposed to Louis XIV a plan... to conquer Egypt. The main purpose of this adventure project was to distract the French king from the destruction of German lands. The plan was rejected, but Napoleon was executed a hundred years later on almost the same route.The philosopher's friends longed to prevent him from meeting, fearing that Leibnitz was a secret spy of the German prince, and Spinoza received a guest, but Leibnitz was ashamed of this visit all his life (because of the difference in religious views) and later denied that he had spoken to him.When he convinced the Duke in 1679 to set up a mining plant, the local people were convinced that Leibnitz was planning to turn lead into gold with the help of hell.\n\nAnd he used to hide money in the wig folds. Contemporarys remembered how, during lively discussions, he suddenly began to dig through his neck to get a coin and show some math drawing on it.He worked in a wheeled chair to ride around the office without getting up. His office was so crumbled with paper that he often fell asleep right at the table, and woke up to write, without making a difference between the hours of the day.He even invented a project to send scientists missionaries to China and painted a coat of arms for the future Chinese Academy of Sciences.\n\nThe King's Black List: When the Hanover Duke became King George I of England, Leibnitz went to London to introduce himself to the monarch. However, the King struck his name off the court list, saying, \"He is too old and harmful.\" A scientist who worked for the Duchess for 40 years stayed in Hanover with nothing.In 1689, Leibnitz visited Rome and was enthralled by ancient statues and sent a letter to his house urgently demanding that he send a hammer and a tooth, and he sincerely believed that he could cut out from the marble the sculpture that repeats antiquities.\n\nThe funeral that wasn't there - the stolen tombstone: since Leibnitz didn't have a family, his funeral was almost invisible. The court didn't come, and the only fact recorded in the church book was that his old secretary was behind the coffin.But the most serious thing happened later: his tombstone was stolen! Many years later, when the church was reconstructed after the war, the builders discovered a gravestone under the roots of an old oak on the outskirts of Hanover, apparently being decided to use it as a material for the bridge.Neither the dates of life, no titles, no titles is just a humble reminder of who lies in the ground. Leibnitz was a unique man: he could lose a carriage during mathematical reflection, argue with kings about the priority of discoveries, and at the same time be interested in alchemy, which was not magic but early chemistry for him. His life is an endless draft of a great book that he never finished."
